WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.630
Twelve has six positive factors: one, two, three, four,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.730
six and twelve.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.100
We can list those by finding the products

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.050
that make twelve.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.520
For a larger number, it is easier to count choices from

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.950
its prime factorisation.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.010
Three hundred and sixty factorises into two cubed,

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.410
three squared and five.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.310
Its full factor list has twenty-four entries.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.010
We can account for them through three small counts,

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.250
one for each prime.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.650
Let's see how this works with twelve first.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.810
Twelve is two times two times three.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.240
A factor of twelve is made from some of

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.230
those prime tiles.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.850
Think about how many twos you can use.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.430
Then think about your choices for the three.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.570
How many different products are possible?

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.620
Use zero, one or two twos.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.100
That gives three choices.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.150
For each of them, either include the three or

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:54.840
leave it out.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.360
That gives two choices.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.100
Three times two makes six products.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.380
Taking no tiles gives one.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.580
Taking all the tiles gives twelve.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.000
Every cell makes a different factor.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.840
Seventy-two is two cubed times three squared.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.260
Choose zero, one, two or three twos.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.430
For each choice there are three possibilities

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:16.480
for the threes.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.180
Four times three gives twelve factors.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.390
For three hundred and sixty, the extra choice about the

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.420
five doubles that count to twenty-four.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.780
For thirty-six, the factor pairs are one and thirty-six,

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:32.480
two and eighteen, three and twelve, and four and nine.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.690
Six pairs with itself, so it appears just once

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:36.400
in the list.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.390
A factor pairs with itself exactly when its square

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:40.280
is the number.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.150
That is why square numbers have an odd count

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.280
of positive factors.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.430
For each prime, include the choice to use zero copies.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.730
Add one to the prime's exponent,

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.690
and multiply the resulting counts.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.340
In your notebook, find the number of positive factors of

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.020
two hundred, then explain why your method counts

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.170
each factor once.
